{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788361684,"nonce":"eS7uYd3Uo842G0WsR3qaoZRYSOw=","message":"**Implemented, worker, repair round 2026-09-02.** Branch `fix/f9dadc7-stalled-build`, commit `c50df7f` (rebased onto `main` at `7fbe7db`). Batched with `f9dadc7` on one branch and one vocabulary, per dispatch.\n\n**The issue's diagnosis holds on every checkable point, and the artifacts prove it harder than the issue does.**\n\n* `RUNS/m1-20260901/journal/20260902T002505.ndjson` seq 42 places `ly-1`'s 33 elements at tick 1023, with `pl-23` = `Heater` at `[111,137]`.\n* 32 of them completed by tick 12687. **`pl-23` completed at tick 60755, worker Lacey** — after `summary.md`'s recorded human intervention raising Lacey's Construction from 4 to 8. The element built the moment, and only the moment, somebody cleared the gate. That is stronger evidence than the quoted envelope.\n* `Buildings_Temperature.xml`: `Heater.constructionSkillPrerequisite 5`, cost list `Steel 50 + ComponentIndustrial 1` — exactly the `missing` row. `Cooler` is 5 as well.\n* **One thing is corroborated rather than read back, and is said so rather than asserted:** the literal \"30 unforbidden reachable ComponentIndustrial\" figure. No save exists inside the Heater's window and the quoted `construction` envelope is not in the artifacts. What IS checkable: the scenario grants exactly 30, and no save carries a `\u003cforbidden\u003e` element on any component stack.\n\n**THE ISSUE'S MECHANISM IS INCOMPLETE, and the correction changes the design.** The issue says \"no pawn can take the job, so `WorkGiver_ConstructDeliverResources` never hauls the component\". True of the Construction-workType giver: `WorkGiver_ConstructDeliverResourcesToBlueprints.JobOnThing` calls `GenConstruct.CanConstruct(blueprint, pawn, def.workType, …)`, and that overload sets `checkSkills: workType == WorkTypeDefOf.Construction`; the Core def has `\u003cworkType\u003eConstruction\u003c/workType\u003e`, so the clause fires.\n\n**But `Core/Defs/WorkGiverDefs/WorkGivers.xml` declares the SAME class a second time** as `DeliverResourcesToBlueprints` / `DeliverResourcesToFrames` with `\u003cworkType\u003eHauling\u003c/workType\u003e`, where `checkSkills` is FALSE — and `WorkGiver_ConstructDeliverResources.IsNewValidNearbyNeeder` passes `checkSkills: false` too. **A hauler can stock a skill-gated blueprint**, leaving a Frame that `ConstructFinishFrames` will never finish.\n\nSo the gate wears **two** wrong costumes, not one: `awaiting-materials` when no hauler reached it, and `ready` when one did. The README's triage table sent an agent down a *different* wrong branch for each — \"unforbid more components\" in the first case, \"check work priorities\" in the second. That is the whole argument for the new token outranking both.\n\n**What shipped.** `ConstructionSkill.cs` asks the game's own question, by member:\n\n* `RimWorld/GenConstruct.cs CanConstruct`'s `checkSkills` branch, read off **`t.def`** — the BLUEPRINT/FRAME def, which is the expression `CanConstruct` itself uses. `ThingDefGenerator_Buildings` copies the field onto the blueprint def `if (!isInstallBlueprint)` and unconditionally onto the frame def, so a `Blueprint_Install` genuinely has NO skill gate and reading the built def would have invented one for every reinstall. Preflights have no thing yet and ask the `BuildableDef`, which is what `Designator_Build` reads.\n* **BOTH prerequisites on ONE colonist.** Two maxima do not decide it (Construction 5 / Artistic 0 beside Construction 0 / Artistic 6 clears a def needing 5 and 6 in neither body). `Designator_Build.DrawPlaceMouseAttachments` loops `FreeColonists` testing both levels on one pawn before drawing `NoColonistWithAllSkillsForConstructing`; this does the same.\n\nItem 1 — `build --dry-run` publishes `skill {construction_required, artistic_required, best_construction, best_artistic, clears[], clears_count, blocked, hint}` plus an envelope-level `skill_basis`. `place-layout` publishes `skill_shortfall[]`, shaped like `shortfall[]` and **always present**, so `[]` means checked-and-clean rather than never-looked. `hint` is a measurement plus the named fix: *\"nobody has Construction 5; the best is Lacey at 4\"*.\n\nItem 2 — **RESOLVED: it does NOT refuse, it places and says so.** The issue argues \"the material shortfall precedent argues for refusing without `--partial`\". Read `LayoutVerbs.PlaceLayout` and the precedent says the opposite: `failed` — the only quantity the place/refuse invariant keys on — counts parse errors, `SiteGate` verdicts and self-overlaps; `MaterialBill` runs *after* it and only fills `data`. This run's own barracks was priced, found 6 steel short, corrected and placed. A shortfall has never refused anything. And a skill ceiling is *less* permanent than a shortage — it is cleared by a colonist levelling up, which happens by building the other 32 elements — so refusing would break \"place the room, build what you can\" for a condition that resolves itself.\n\nItem 3 — new state token **`no-builder`**, precedence `blocked \u003e in-progress \u003e no-builder \u003e awaiting-materials \u003e ready`. Below `blocked` because `CanConstruct` tests `FirstBlockingThing` before the skill clause (the game's own order); below `in-progress` because a pawn with a job on the thing is a true statement about this tick — but the `skill` block is published on the row in **every** state, so the fact never disappears while that is true. `digest.construction` gains `no_builder` (state count) and `skill_blocked` (verdict count, which does not flicker when a hauler touches it; `skill_blocked \u003e no_builder` means something is being worked that can never complete).\n\nItem 4 — `rwa/README.md`'s triage becomes a five-row table with the fourth branch and its lever; `checklists/triggered.md` gains `somebody-can-actually-build-it`, act-keyed after any `place-layout` / `build`.\n\nItem 5 — `accept/e08c3e5-skill-prereq.py`. The fixture is `TrapSpike` (prerequisite 3, **no research prerequisite**, 1x1, stuff-based), not `Heater` — a Heater fixture would test the `Electricity` research gate as much as the skill one. Phase 9 asserts that property of the def rather than trusting it.\n\n**Open questions from the issue, answered:**\n\n* **`p.IsColonyMech`** — out of scope for M1 as the issue says, and the implementation reads only the humanlike roster (`MapPawns.FreeColonists` is Humanlike-only). This is NOT silent: `skill_basis.not_asked` says in the envelope that `CanConstruct`'s mech branch reads `RaceProps.mechFixedSkillLevel` and that `Designator_Build.AnyMechWithSkillsRequired` asks `MechanitorUtility.AnyPlayerMechCanDoWork`, so **a mech colony's real ceiling can be HIGHER than reported**. Phase 1 asserts that sentence is present.\n* **Work settings** — deliberately NOT conflated. `CanConstruct(Thing, Pawn, WorkTypeDef, …)` refuses first on `!pawn.workSettings.WorkIsActive(workType)`, and `Designator_Build.AnyColonistWithSkill` takes `careIfDisabled` as a parameter precisely because the two questions differ. A colonist who HAS the skill with Construction switched off is the README's existing work-priority branch and must not be dressed up as a skill ceiling. Also in `not_asked`.\n* **`artisticSkillPrerequisite`** — implemented and read from the same place as its twin, but **NO def in the entire shipped Data tree sets it to a nonzero value.** Grepped, unpiped, across `Core` and every DLC. So the twin clause has no vanilla fixture and nothing live can exercise it; the suite reports this as a FINDING rather than fabricating a test. Coverage needs a modded def.\n* **`TerrainDef`** — `TerrainTemplateDef` carries the field (SterileTile 6, stone tiles 3) and `ThingDefGenerator_Buildings` copies it onto the generated terrain blueprint def, so reading `t.def` SHOULD cover floors. Nothing here places a floor. **Unproven**, said in the suite's header.\n\n**NOT DEMONSTRATED, in those words.** No bench was touched — the worker launches nothing. Offline: 25/25 including the run's journal cross-check that `pl-23` completed at tick 60755. Live phases 1-6 are the orchestrator's to run.","files":null}]}